    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d190">

                <pb facs="0361" n="273" xml:id="pg273"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">763.72119/3116</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Italian Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Macchi di Cellere</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Counselor for the Department of State</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Polk</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1918-11-19">November 19,
                            1918</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Polk</hi>: In connection with our
                    conversation of yesterday, I take pleasure in transmitting to you a memorandum
                    regarding the situation in Tripoli arising out of the violation of the armistice
                    by Turkey,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d190-f1"/> and the necessity of putting an end
                    to it. I shall appreciate it if the American Government will approach the
                    British Government with the view of having Turkey keep faith to her
                    engagements.</p>
                <p>With appreciation [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Macchi di Cellere</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d190-a1.xml" xpointer="d190-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d190-f1" n="2" xml:id="d190-f1-content">
                <p>For terms of the Anglo-Turkish armistice, see undated telegram No. 32 from
                    Colonel House, <ref target="frus1918Supp01v01#pg_441">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1918, supp. 1, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 441</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
